{"records":[{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4g722x4jfg","title":"Western blot","description":"Western blot of CRISPR-Cas9-mediated nontargeting (NT) versus BACH2 knockout 3 days after CRISPR-Cas9-gRNA nucleofection of human gut cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-02T21:20:55.966Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4g722x4jfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4g722x4jfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yulong","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut","Western Blot","T Cell","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3ctpx4k6jp","title":"Porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus upregulates SMPDL3B to promote viral replication by modulating lipid metabolism","description":"We generated a collection of porcine genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 knockout (PigGeCKO) cells on a PAM background.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-26T18:53:54.241Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3ctpx4k6jp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3ctpx4k6jp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"huanh","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Agricultural Biotechnology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"33tm3dd9dw","title":"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery ","description":"Raw data deposited for \"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery\" by Djamshidi et al-Cell Reports Methods","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-25T15:53:30.358Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/33tm3dd9dw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/33tm3dd9dw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mahbod","last_name":"Djamshidi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Sanger Sequencing","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7ch42j6767","title":"Long term expandable mouse and human induced nephron progenitor cells enable organoid modeling of kidney maturation, plasticity and disease","description":"Nephron progenitor cells (NPCs) self-renew and differentiate into nephrons, the functional unit of the kidney. Here, manipulation of p38 and YAP activity allowed for long-term clonal expansion of primary mouse and human NPCs, and induced NPCs (iNPCs) from human pluripotent stem cells. Molecular analyses demonstrated cultured iNPCs resemble closely primary human NPCs. iNPCs generated nephron organoids with minimal off-target cell types and enhanced maturation of podocytes relative to published human kidney organoid protocols. Surprisingly, NPC culture medium uncovered plasticity in human podocyte programs, enabling podocyte reprogramming to an NPC-like state. Scalability and ease of genome-editing facilitated genome-wide CRISPR screening in NPC culture, uncovering novel genes associated with kidney development and dis-ease. Further, NPC-directed modeling of autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) identified a small molecule inhibitor of cystogenesis. These findings highlight a broad application for the reported iNPC platform in the study of kidney development, disease, plasticity, and regeneration. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-15T14:23:53.448Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7ch42j6767","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7ch42j6767"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BIAO","last_name":"HUANG","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zipeng","last_name":"Zeng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sunghyun ","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Connor-James ","last_name":"Fausto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kari ","last_name":"Koppitch","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kenneth","last_name":"Hallows","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nils","last_name":"Lindstrom","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"McMahon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhongwei","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gel Electrophoresis","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vdf4nyfd9y","title":"Video supplements for Project Grant application (Theme3)","description":"Video 1, cardiomyocytes (hESC-CM) differentiated from Control CRISPR hESC cells at Day8. \nVideo 2, cardiomyocytes (hESC-CM) differentiated from STX18-AS1 CRISPR hESC cells at Day8. \nVideo 3, endocardial cells (hESC-EEC) differentiation from hESC at Day 10.\nVideo 4, beating organoid with multiple cardiac cell lineages (hESC derived)  after 2 weeks of culture. \nVideo 5, beating organoid with multiple cardiac cell lineages (hESC derived)  after 6 weeks of culture. ","source":{"id":"c41270fd-87bf-4be5-8668-b8aa8f867463","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-27T06:14:42.957Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vdf4nyfd9y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vdf4nyfd9y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yingjuan","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Video","Grant"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xvk2gk4hw3","title":"Data of Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","description":"Raw data for figures in Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-25T15:27:04.321Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xvk2gk4hw3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xvk2gk4hw3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Developmental Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hzr4fnvc88","title":"Rapid two-step target capture ensures efficient CRISPR-Cas9-guided genome editing. Shi, Al-Sayyad, & Wasko et al.","description":"Raw experimental data from experiments detailed in \"Rapid two-step target capture ensures efficient CRISPR-Cas9-guided genome editing.\" by Shi, Al-Sayyad, & Wasko et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-15T13:22:36.896Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hzr4fnvc88","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hzr4fnvc88"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kevin","last_name":"Wasko","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Enzymatic Activity","Genome Editing","Molecular Biophysics"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":135194,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":350,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135194,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":134504,"restricted":67,"embargoed":623},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2266},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1332},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":655},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":474},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":416},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for S